  "intro": "Parametric equations give $x=x(t)$ and $y=y(t)$, tracing a curve as $t$ varies. The slope is $\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\dfrac{dy/dt}{dx/dt}$ (where $dx/dt\\ne 0$). The second derivative is $\\frac{d^2y}{dx^2}=\\dfrac{d}{dt}\\!\\left(\\frac{dy}{dx}\\right)\\big/\\frac{dx}{dt}$. Arc length is $L=\\int_a^b\\sqrt{(dx/dt)^2+(dy/dt)^2}\\,dt$. You can also eliminate the parameter to get a Cartesian equation.",
  "items": [
    {
      "type": "worked-example",
      "id": 1,
      "prompt": "For $x=t^2$, $y=t^3$, find $\\dfrac{dy}{dx}$.",
      "solution": {
        "steps": [
          "Compute $\\frac{dx}{dt}=2t$ and $\\frac{dy}{dt}=3t^2$.",
          "Divide: $\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{3t^2}{2t}$.",
          "Simplify for $t\\ne 0$: $\\frac{3t}{2}$."
        ],
        "answer": "$\\dfrac{dy}{dx}=\\dfrac{3t}{2}$"
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "worked-example",
      "id": 2,
      "prompt": "Eliminate the parameter: $x=2t$, $y=t^2$.",
      "solution": {
        "steps": [
          "Solve the first for $t$: $t=\\frac{x}{2}$.",
          "Substitute into $y=t^2$: $y=\\left(\\frac{x}{2}\\right)^2=\\frac{x^2}{4}$.",
          "So the curve is the parabola $y=\\frac{x^2}{4}$."
        ],
        "answer": "$y=\\dfrac{x^2}{4}$"
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "worked-example",
      "id": 3,
      "prompt": "Find the arc length of $x=\\cos t$, $y=\\sin t$ for $0\\le t\\le 2\\pi$.",
      "solution": {
        "steps": [
          "$\\frac{dx}{dt}=-\\sin t$, $\\frac{dy}{dt}=\\cos t$.",
          "$(dx/dt)^2+(dy/dt)^2=\\sin^2 t+\\cos^2 t=1$.",
          "$L=\\int_0^{2\\pi}\\sqrt{1}\\,dt=2\\pi$ (the circumference of the unit circle)."
        ],
        "answer": "$L=2\\pi$"
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "practice",
      "id": 4,
      "prompt": "For $x=t$, $y=t^2$, find $\\frac{dy}{dx}$.",
      "solution": {
        "steps": [
          "$\\frac{dy/dt}{dx/dt}=\\frac{2t}{1}=2t$."
        ],
        "answer": "$2t$"
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "practice",
      "id": 5,
      "prompt": "Eliminate the parameter: $x=t+1$, $y=t^2$.",
      "solution": {
        "steps": [
          "$t=x-1$; $y=(x-1)^2$."
        ],
        "answer": "$y=(x-1)^2$"
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "practice",
      "id": 6,
      "prompt": "Slope of $x=\\cos t$, $y=\\sin t$ at $t=\\frac{\\pi}{4}$.",
      "solution": {
        "steps": [
          "$\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{\\cos t}{-\\sin t}=-\\cot t$; at $\\frac{\\pi}{4}$, $-1$."
        ],
        "answer": "$-1$"
      }
    }
  ]
